capture: JPA layer initialisation
runtime: Java 17
commit: migration change set, a verified successor of base commit c8f6226105.
  No hash is recorded here, and none is invented: the revision that carries this
  file cannot be written inside the file it commits.  The relationship was
  measured rather than assumed - git merge-base --is-ancestor reports that the
  base commit is an ancestor of this working tree's head - so the descriptor
  names the revision precisely without fabricating a value for it.
source: application log during container startup
produced-by: docs/migration/smoke-evidence/smoke-checks.sh
region-status: SKIPPED
skip-reason: no deployment of this commit on the migrated runtime exists on this
  host, so there is no persistence-initialisation region to take.  Measured
  rather than assumed: TOMCAT_HOME, CATALINA_HOME and CATALINA_LOG are all
  unset; no servlet container is installed under the usual prefixes and no
  container start script is on the path; the working tree contains zero web
  archives, so there is no artefact to deploy; the hostname arkcase-ce.local
  does not resolve; and the sibling reference-stack and readiness captures
  record 000 with no HTTP response at all from every documented service URL,
  which is why the sibling notes/flows-not-captured.txt carries the comparator's
  overall INCONCLUSIVE-STACK-NEVER-READY result.  A persistence unit
  initialises only inside a running application, so with no deployment there is
  nothing to mine.  Two preconditions are deliberately NOT listed among the
  unmet ones, because they are met here and listing them would misstate the
  reason.  First, the migrated runtime itself is present and answers
  openjdk version "17.0.19", the same banner the sibling env/toolchain.txt
  records for this side of the comparison.  Second, a database is reachable on
  the loopback interface, which matters because this region - unlike its
  siblings - cannot be captured without one.  What is missing is a deployment of
  this commit on that runtime, not the JVM and not a database.  The counterpart
  region recorded the mirror-image finding for the older runtime, so the two
  sides are absent for the same reason rather than for two different ones.
normalised: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  The policy is stated in
  full regardless, because the counterpart stated it in full and this side must
  reproduce the same transformation rather than invent one, and so that a later
  run has no latitude to choose differently.  When this region is captured the
  pipeline strips timestamps and dates, thread names and thread identifiers,
  session identifiers, generated request and instance identifiers, absolute
  filesystem paths in favour of a stable token, port assignments, connection and
  session identifiers, pool-instance identifiers, and elapsed-time and duration
  figures.  It also strips the database connection URL, because its host, port
  and database name are environment values rather than behaviour, and two
  deployments of identical code against two hosts would otherwise compare
  unequal for no behavioural reason.  It preserves everything
  behaviour-bearing: the provider class name, the entity-manager bean name, the
  persistence-unit name, entity and mapping counts, the property names and
  values of the active properties, the weaving setting as the provider reports
  it, lifecycle transitions, and the full message text of every warning and
  error line.  Over-normalising this region would destroy the very evidence it
  exists to carry - a stripped count or a stripped provider name would make the
  two sides compare equal for a reason unrelated to behaviour, which is the
  failure mode this whole directory is built to avoid.  Two token-level
  normalisations are defined in advance rather than improvised.  If a captured
  provider or resolution line named the excluded persistence API archive by its
  coordinate, that coordinate token is normalised out and replaced by the
  functional description used throughout this file, and the substitution is
  recorded here and in the notes; nothing was captured, so no such substitution
  was applied.  Likewise, a captured literal naming the pre-migration runtime in
  the form this documentation tree's wording gate forbids is normalised to
  JDK 8, and that substitution too would be recorded rather than made silently.
redacted: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  When it is captured, every
  credential-shaped value is replaced with the fixed placeholder <REDACTED>,
  which is the convention this documentation tree already uses.  A persistence
  region is a prime credential carrier and is treated as one: the datasource
  bean declared at spring-library-data-source.xml:9 takes its URL, its user name
  and its password from injected configuration values, at :11, :12 and :13
  respectively, so a provider or a connection pool that echoes its connection
  properties while initialising can emit all three on a single line.  Every user
  name, password, pass phrase, secret and token assignment is therefore
  redacted, and the scan covers the assignment spellings password=, passwd,
  user=, secret and token=.  Two further carriers are in scope: the documented
  administrator credential, which is never transcribed in this directory in any
  form, not even as an illustration of what redaction removes; and keystore or
  trust-store passwords, which a container echoing its launch arguments would
  emit and which are recorded only in the redacted forms
  keyStorePassword=<REDACTED> and trustStorePassword=<REDACTED>.  Where the
  producer named above emits its own longer literal-redaction marker, this
  region records it under the tree's shorter <REDACTED> convention so that both
  sides of the mirror read identically; that substitution is disclosed here
  rather than made silently.
