notes:
- The capture delimiters above are deliberately empty.  Every capture in this
  directory is script-produced; a hand-authored capture is not evidence.
  Because the preconditions recorded in skip-reason were not available, no log
  line was invented to fill the gap.  Fabrication would be worse here than
  anywhere else in this directory, and the reason is structural rather than
  stylistic: BOTH claims this region exists to carry are NEGATIVE claims - that
  no schema was modified, and that no duplicate-class warning was emitted for
  the persistence API package.  An invented capture would "prove" both of them
  trivially, by containing nothing incriminating, and an invented clean
  persistence region is indistinguishable from a real one.  That makes it
  worthless as evidence and actively misleading as a document.  An explicit skip
  preserves exactly the thing a fabrication destroys: the reviewer's ability to
  tell the difference.  The governing rule that nothing be silently skipped is
  satisfied by a machine-readable skip with a specific reason beside it; it is
  not satisfied by an invented capture.
- A SKIPPED region is NOT a pass, and two matching SKIPPED regions across
  baseline and migrated are NOT evidence of preserved behaviour.  They record an
  absence in both places.  Nothing in this file may be read as confirming that
  persistence initialised on the migrated runtime, that the schema was untouched
  at run time, or that no duplicate-class warning was emitted.  The findings
  below are of two kinds and are labelled individually: MEASURED findings, which
  are properties of this repository and of its resolved dependency graph and
  were derived by running the command shown beside each one; and
  expected-evidence findings, which are expectations about what a captured
  region would contain and were witnessed by nobody.  No MEASURED finding is a
  run-time observation, and no expected-evidence finding is an observation of
  any kind.
- produced-by names the designated producer of this region, not a claim that the
  producer emitted content on this run.  It emitted none, which is why the
  delimiters are empty.  Two independent reasons: the container log the producer
  mines was absent, and the producer also refuses to start without an
  administrator credential supplied through its environment, so no run was
  possible here even setting the absent deployment aside.  Supplying
  administrator material would have been neither available nor appropriate.  The
  producer's own startup-region routine writes startup/unavailable.log when no
  container log is supplied, and exactly that file - byte-identical on both
  sides of the comparison - records the reason as CATALINA_LOG being unset or
  not naming a readable file.  The absence is therefore recorded twice, by two
  different mechanisms, and the two records agree with each other.
- Region relationship to the producer, recorded so the mirror is reproducible.
  The producer's own startup-region set folds broker and persistence lines into
  a single combined region, selected by one pattern.  This file is the
  persistence-only narrowing of that same startup window: broker and messaging
  lines belong to the messaging region and are not duplicated here, and nothing
  in this file describes messaging behaviour.
- Region-selection rule, identical to the rule the counterpart region states, so
  that the two sides selected the same window rather than merely claiming to.
  Select, from the same first-startup window the sibling container-startup
  region covers, exactly the lines belonging to persistence initialisation: the
  persistence provider announcing itself, the persistence unit being built and
  reaching a working state, entity and mapping counts, the weaving setting as
  the provider reports it, the login and schema-context transitions of the
  persistence session, and every persistence warning and error line as observed.
  The window opens at the first provider or persistence-unit line and closes
  when the persistence session is in a working state, and the whole window is in
  scope.  Selecting a narrower window is the easiest way to produce a region
  that looks clean because the interesting part had not happened yet - and in a
  region whose claims are negative, a truncated window is not merely incomplete,
  it is affirmatively misleading.
- Territory boundaries, so the mirror diff stays mechanical and nothing is
  double-counted.  This region covers persistence initialisation only.
  Container and application startup belongs to the sibling catalina.out.log;
  Spring context initialisation and aspect wiring to spring-context.log,
  annotation scanning to reflection-scan.log, process-engine initialisation to
  workflow-engine-init.log, the startup asset build to frontend-build.log, and
  readiness polling to readiness-poll.log; the consolidated error-severity scan
  is error-scan.txt, which reads from this same window and is the place a reader
  goes for every severity line at once.  Each of those is named here as the
  designated home for its subject matter, and none of them is duplicated here.
  Toolchain provenance belongs to the sibling env/toolchain.txt, artefact
  digests to the artifacts directory - no digest value is written here and none
  is computed here - archived unit-test reports to the surefire directory, and
  the eight application flows to the flow captures.  Equally, this file carries
  no SQL statement body, no schema definition and no dependency inventory: it is
  a startup-log region, and a schema dump or a dependency table here would both
  exceed its remit and invite a reader to treat a table as evidence of a run.
- The archive directory is startup/, and that name is load-bearing rather than
  cosmetic.  The repository's root ignore file uses bare, leading-slash-free
  patterns, and git matches those at any depth.  Re-verified at THIS directory's
  exact depth rather than taken on trust, with a .log file inside each
  candidate: a file under logs resolves to IGNORED by the pattern on line 26,
  under bin by line 7, under target by line 6, under builds by line 40, under
  activemq-data by line 25 and under partition-descriptor by line 24, while this
  file's own path resolves to no match and is committable.  The ignored token is
  always the DIRECTORY name and never the .log extension, so a .log file placed
  inside a directory named logs would be silently uncommitted while every local
  check still passed.  This file therefore sits directly in startup/ and no
  subdirectory was created beneath it.
- Anchor convention, stated so the two sides of the comparison can be read
  together without confusion.  Every README.md and docs/setup.md anchor in this
  file was re-read in this migrated tree and cites the line numbers that hold
  here.  The counterpart region cites the same content at the base commit, where
  the numbers differ, because those documents were edited by this migration and
  the surrounding prose reflowed.  Neither set of anchors is wrong; each is given
  at the revision its own region describes, which is the only revision at which
  that region is meaningful.  The data-source configuration this region depends
  on is the fortunate case and needs no such care: it is byte-identical between
  the base commit and this tree, which was verified rather than assumed, so
  every configuration anchor below resolves at either revision.
- MEASURED: the persistence provider is configured explicitly and its identity
  does not change in this migration.  At spring-library-data-source.xml:49 the
  bean acmEntityManager is declared as
  org.springframework.orm.jpa.LocalContainerEntityManagerFactoryBean, and at :52
  its persistenceProviderClass property is set to
  org.eclipse.persistence.jpa.PersistenceProvider.  The provider is therefore
  named in configuration rather than discovered, which is why the provider name
  is preserved through normalisation rather than stripped: it is the one value in
  the region that says which implementation actually ran.  Only the provider's
  version advances; the provider itself is the same one on both sides of the
  mirror, and a region naming a different provider would be a finding rather
  than a difference.  The reason for the version advance, which the rule
  requiring a specific justification for every version change demands: the
  installed release predates the target runtime by six years, and the line
  chosen is the last one that keeps the existing API namespace - the successor
  generation moves to the successor namespace and is excluded by directive.
  Two further wirings on the same bean complete the picture and are unchanged by
  this migration.  At :51 the jpaDialect property is bound by reference to the
  provider-specific dialect bean, which is the seam through which the framework
  asks the provider for transaction and exception behaviour rather than
  implementing either itself; and the datasource property that would normally sit
  beside it at :50 is commented out, so this bean takes its connection source
  from the persistence unit manager wired at :81 instead.  Both are recorded
  exactly as they are.
- MEASURED, and worth its own note: dynamic weaving is DISABLED, and it stays
  disabled.  Line :55 reads exactly the property key eclipselink.weaving with
  the value false.  Re-derived rather than taken on trust, and the result
  diverges from what both the migration plan and the counterpart region record,
  so the divergence is stated rather than glossed.  The command
  grep -rn 'eclipselink.weaving' --include=*.xml . returns TWO hits on this
  working tree, not one.  Both are line :55 and both carry the value false.  The
  second is a build-output copy of the same file beneath a target directory,
  which is untracked and is ignored by the root ignore file's pattern on line 6;
  it exists only because a Maven build ran in this workspace.  Restricted to
  tracked files the same search returns EXACTLY ONE hit, the configuration line
  itself, and it remains the only weaving reference in any tracked XML file in
  the tree.  So the plan's figure is right about the source and the raw command
  is right about the working directory, and the difference is a build artefact
  rather than a change in configuration.  This matters more than its one line
  suggests.  Weaving is where a persistence provider manipulates entity bytecode
  as classes load, and it is the persistence layer's single largest
  bytecode-related risk on a new class file version - precisely the class of
  failure a provider advance would be expected to produce.  With weaving off the
  provider never rewrites an entity class, and the provider advance carries
  materially less risk than it otherwise would.  Two qualifications keep this
  honest.  It is a PRE-EXISTING property of the configuration, present at the
  base commit and not something this migration introduced, arranged or may take
  credit for; claiming it as a migration achievement would misrepresent the
  change set.  And it is a reduction of risk, not a proof of its absence,
  because a provider still reads entity metadata reflectively whether or not it
  weaves.
- MEASURED, and this is the first of the two claims this region exists to carry:
  NO SCHEMA MODIFICATION.  The evidence is an ABSENCE, which is harder to
  present honestly than a positive result, so both halves are given - what was
  looked for, and what looking returned.  The whole property map spanning :53 to
  :78 contains no schema-generation property at all.  Re-derived rather than
  taken on trust: the command
  grep -rn 'ddl-generation' --include=*.xml . returns 0.  Stating the command
  beside the result is the point of this note, because a reader cannot otherwise
  distinguish "the schema was not modified" from "nobody looked".  One
  self-reference is disclosed rather than glossed, because a claim a document
  falsifies by existing is worse than no claim at all: widening the same search
  to every file type in the tree returns hits only in this evidence directory -
  the counterpart region, and now this file - each of which necessarily contains
  the property name because it quotes the command.  Run with this evidence
  directory excluded, the widened form also returns 0.  A provider told nothing
  about schema generation does not create, drop or alter schema; it maps onto
  whatever schema it finds.  The active property set is exactly SEVEN entries and
  is listed here so a reader can confirm the absence for themselves rather than
  trusting the summary: eclipselink.weaving at :55 set false,
  eclipselink.logging.level at :56, eclipselink.logging.logger at :57,
  eclipselink.cache.shared.default at :63 set false,
  eclipselink.persistence-context.close-on-commit at :64 set true,
  eclipselink.validate-existence at :65 set true, and
  eclipselink.jdbc.sequence-connection-pool at :66 set true.  Every
  connection-pool property from :67 to :76 is commented out, including the three
  at :71, :72 and :73 that would otherwise carry a connection URL, a user name
  and a password - which is why those three are named here as commented rather
  than quoted; their values are not transcribed, and the fact that they are
  inert does not change that.  The bean closes by wiring jpaVendorAdapter at :80
  and persistenceUnitManager at :81, neither of which introduces a
  schema-generation setting.  What a captured region must therefore show is the
  persistence unit initialising against the EXISTING schema with no DDL
  statement of any kind anywhere in the window; the absence of DDL output is
  the evidence, and it is evidence only because the search for it is recorded.
  Nothing in this file implies any schema change or any change to the effective
  definition a persistence mapping generates.
- MEASURED, and this is the second claim: NO DUPLICATE-CLASS WARNING for the
  persistence API package.  The migration keeps exactly ONE persistence API
  archive on the classpath.  The provider advance would otherwise bring in a
  second copy - the same persistence API republished under a different
  artifactId, which is excluded for that reason - and the two are
  content-identical, so carrying both would place every one of their classes on
  the classpath twice, under order-dependent nearest-wins resolution that is not
  reproducible between two builds.  Three things were measured rather than
  accepted.  First, the content identity: both archives were listed from the
  local artefact cache with the archive tool the JDK already ships and their
  sorted class-entry lists compared with comm.  Each archive holds 213 class
  entries, 209 of them under the persistence API package and the remaining 4
  under an OSGi provider-resolver area, and the symmetric difference between the
  two sets is EMPTY - zero classes unique to either side, 213 common to both.
  So the duplication avoided is 213 classes, 209 of them in the persistence API
  package itself.  Divergence recorded rather than passed over: the migration
  plan states 211 for this figure, where the comparison above yields 213 total
  and 209 within the package; the conclusion is unaffected and is what matters -
  the two archives are the same API twice over, and only one of them is on the
  classpath.  Second, neither archive carries a single class under the successor
  top-level package prefix, which was re-derived too and is the whole point:
  artefact eligibility in this migration is decided by inspecting an archive's
  package prefixes AND its coordinates, not by reading either alone, which is why
  an archive whose classes all carry the retained package prefix is still
  excluded when its coordinates do not.  Third, that the exclusion actually
  HOLDS: resolving the dependency graph of the module this configuration lives in
  lists exactly one archive of the persistence API, at the API level the provider
  implements, and zero occurrences of the excluded one.  One distinction matters
  for anyone reproducing this and is easy to trip over: the excluded archive IS
  present in the local artefact cache, because dependency mediation resolves and
  caches an archive even when an exclusion keeps it off a module's classpath.
  Presence in the cache is not presence on the classpath, and only the latter
  would produce a duplicate.  These three findings are build-configuration
  facts about the resolved graph; they are NOT a run-time observation, and they
  do not substitute for one.  The observable consequence, and the thing to look
  for in the captured region, is the absence of any duplicate-class or
  split-package warning naming the persistence API package.  Its absence
  evidences that the targeted exclusion held and that resolution was
  deterministic; its presence on either side would be a finding.
- MEASURED, in one line because it is a build-configuration fact and this region
  is not the place for a dependency essay: the provider's class-file-reading
  companion has no release at the provider's target version - its older line
  stops several patches earlier and then jumps to a different major series - so
  the family was split into two version axes, and sharing one property across it
  would have failed resolution outright.
- Capture precondition, recorded honestly rather than presented as a clean
  result.  The provider's logging level and logger are CONFIGURABLE, not fixed:
  :56 and :57 take their values from injected configuration rather than
  literals.  That configurability is why this region is capturable at all - a
  provider logging at a fine level announces its persistence unit, its counts
  and its session transitions, and a region can be mined from that.  It cuts the
  other way just as sharply.  If the level is set coarsely in the environment
  under capture, the region will be THIN or even empty through configuration
  alone, and a thin region must be reported AS THIN rather than read as evidence
  that nothing went wrong: "few lines" is not "nothing happened", and a reviewer
  must be able to tell which was observed.  Nothing was captured here at all, so
  this side is neither thin nor full - it is absent, which is a third state and
  is recorded as such in region-status.  When the region is captured, the level
  in force must be stated beside it, and both sides of the mirror must be
  captured at the same configured level; two regions taken at different levels
  would differ for a reason that carries no behavioural signal.
- MEASURED: the persistence-annotation surface is enormous and entirely
  untouched, which is the reason a provider and API advance can leave the source
  alone.  Re-derived here rather than quoted from the plan.  The command
  grep -rn 'javax.persistence' --include=*.java . piped through
  grep -c '/src/main/java/' returns 1612 main-source occurrences, spread over 255
  distinct main-source files; the criteria sub-package, counted the same way,
  returns 69.  Divergence recorded rather than passed over: the plan states
  1,610 for the first figure, where the command above returns 1612 on this tree;
  the criteria figure of 69 matches the plan exactly.  Both figures are identical
  at the base commit and in this migrated tree, measured both ways, so the
  divergence is a counting difference and not a change in the source - and that
  identity is itself the finding: zero import edits were made across that
  surface.  The API advance adds exactly two annotation-container types over the
  release previously in use, both absent from the source, so the source impact is
  zero and the impact on the effective definition a mapping generates is zero.
  This is the preserve-list obligation on the database schema and on the
  effective DDL of persistence mappings, discharged by measurement.  No
  entity-by-entity mapping detail is enumerated here, deliberately: the claim is
  that the mappings are unchanged, and listing 1612 occurrences would obscure
  that claim rather than support it.
- expected-evidence: warning lines are expected in a healthy first startup of
  this application and are captured as observed, with their message text
  preserved in full and nothing repaired, retried into submission or hidden -
  including a warning that would make the region read less cleanly.  This region
  carries the persistence ones; the consolidated scan across every severity line
  is the sibling error-scan region, which reads from the same window, which is
  why the two must be drawn from one capture.
- Governing rule on discovered defects, verbatim: "Pre-existing bugs discovered
  during the work are documented rather than fixed, unless one blocks a
  validation item."  Applied to this region that means every persistence warning
  is captured exactly as observed, with nothing repaired, nothing retried into
  submission and nothing hidden.  It also governs the configuration this region
  describes.  The datasource property of the entity-manager bean at :50 is
  COMMENTED OUT, and it is recorded here as commented and left exactly as it is;
  this file neither repairs it, nor proposes repairing it, nor implies that it
  ought to be repaired.  The same applies to every commented connection-pool
  property from :67 to :76.  The migration's escape clause is invoked exactly
  twice in the entire change set and NEITHER invocation is in this folder, so
  nothing here may rely on it.  Nothing in this file proposes, implies or
  performs a fix to any pre-existing condition.
- Governing rule on evidence, verbatim: "Validation asserts on produced
  artifacts and captured output, never on process exit status alone. This is
  mandatory rather than stylistic because Gruntfile.js sets
  grunt.option('force', true), which masks task failures and lets a broken build
  exit zero."  Applied here, and it bites harder in this region than the rule's
  own frontend justification suggests: successful persistence initialisation is
  asserted on the captured log region and on nothing else.  A deployment's exit
  status is not evidence.  A healthy readiness probe is not evidence.  A context
  that finishes starting is not evidence either, and neither is a persistence
  unit that reaches a working state - it can reach one and still have emitted the
  warning that matters.  A persistence unit that "initialises" is not the claim
  this region makes; the captured absence of a DDL statement and of a
  duplicate-class warning is.  The region, not the outcome, is the artefact.
- Governing rule on ambiguity, verbatim: "The application's observed behavior at
  the base commit on JDK 8 is the tie-breaker for any ambiguity, and each
  resolution must be documented."
  [Editorial note: the rule's own text names the pre-migration runtime with a
  phrase this documentation tree's wording gate forbids; the substitution to
  JDK 8 changes no meaning and is disclosed here rather than made silently.]
  That rule is why this file exists at all - it is the migrated half of the
  comparison the rule requires - and it is why an absence is recorded here
  instead of a plausible-looking substitute: an unrun persistence
  initialisation's behaviour is unknowable rather than assumable.  It is also the
  rule that settled the hardest call in this region's subject matter, and the
  three rejected alternatives are recorded so the decision can be audited rather
  than re-litigated.  Keeping the older pinned API would have starved the
  advanced provider of API classes it expects.  Accepting the second,
  transitively-arriving copy would have put an archive on the classpath whose
  coordinates carry the excluded name, which the directive forbids at the
  artefact level and not merely at the import level.  Keeping both would have
  left the duplicate class set resolved order-dependently and therefore
  irreproducibly.  The pinned copy was advanced and the second one excluded
  instead, so that exactly one archive is on the classpath and the effective
  definition every persistence mapping generates is preserved.
- Governing rule on namespace invariance, which bites harder in this region than
  anywhere else in this directory, and which is stated here in this file's own
  words rather than quoted.  Disclosed rather than done silently: the rule's own
  text names the excluded namespace, and this documentation tree's token gate
  excludes that name outright, so reproducing the rule verbatim is impossible
  here and a paraphrase carrying its full prescriptive force is used instead.
  Nothing is softened by the substitution.  What the rule requires: every import
  in the retained platform namespace stays in that namespace; the platform
  modules the JDK removed are compensated for by ADDING artefacts in that same
  retained namespace, never by rewriting source into the successor namespace; and
  an artefact's eligibility is decided by inspecting the archive's package
  prefixes rather than by reading its group or artefact name alone.  This
  region's contribution to that rule is the duplicate-class finding above: one
  persistence API archive, retained namespace, deterministic resolution - and an
  excluded archive that would have satisfied a prefix-only reading of the rule
  while failing a coordinate-level one, which is precisely why both readings are
  applied together.
- Governing rule on skipped work, applied in the only way that is honest here:
  nothing is silently skipped.  This region could not be captured, so it says so
  in a machine-readable field, names the specific unmet precondition, and keeps
  both delimiters present with nothing between them.  The related obligation on
  tests is not this file's to discharge, but the same principle governs: an
  absence is recorded, never quietly omitted, and it is recorded in a form a
  recursive diff of the two capture directories will surface rather than in a
  form indistinguishable from an empty file.
- Governing rule on justified change, applied to how this file was produced as
  well as to what it says: a change without a reason is out of scope.  Every
  figure published above was re-derived on this tree with the command shown
  beside it, using only the POSIX toolset the surrounding evidence uses together
  with the archive tool the JDK already ships - no data-format processor, no
  scripting runtime, no test framework and no package was installed to produce
  this file.  Where a re-derived figure differs from the migration plan's, both
  are stated and the divergence is named; that happened three times here, at the
  weaving hit count, the duplicate class count and the annotation-surface count.
  A figure that could not be re-derived would have been omitted rather than
  repeated on trust.
- Governing rule on production launch configuration is paraphrased here and never
  quoted, deliberately: its own text names a module-access JVM argument
  literally, and this documentation tree is checked by a recursive audit that
  requires zero such literals, so quoting the rule would introduce the very token
  the audit forbids.  The rule requires that production launch configuration not
  depend on JDK internal access - that is, on a JVM argument that opens or
  exports an otherwise-encapsulated JDK package to the unnamed module - except
  where a pinned third-party dependency documentedly requires it, and that every
  such exception be documented.  The register of applied exceptions is referred
  to here BY TITLE ONLY, as the JDK access exceptions register, because its
  filename embeds that token; it is delivered empty with a positive statement
  that none are required.  Its emptiness is an earned result rather than an
  assumption: every strong-encapsulation failure found during this migration was
  inside a test library and was resolved by upgrading or removing the dependency
  that failed rather than by relaxing a module boundary, the application's own
  reflective code targets only application classes, and no security manager is
  installed anywhere.  The persistence layer is the strongest single case for
  that conclusion, because the one place a persistence provider would plausibly
  need a relaxed module boundary is when it rewrites entity bytecode as classes
  load, and that behaviour is switched off in this configuration.
- Provenance of the rules quoted and paraphrased above, and both facts belong
  together because either one alone misleads.  First, the project's rules
  facility reports, verbatim, "No user rules provided." - there is no on-disk
  user rules document for this project, so there is no external full text to
  defer to, and that is explicitly NOT licence to lower the bar.  Second, rules
  are nonetheless present and binding: the requirements embed an explicit,
  numbered block of seven rules that governs this work in full exactly as an
  external rules document would, alongside seven transformation rules.  The
  identifiers the migration plan uses for those fourteen are its OWN
  navigational convention rather than quoted titles, which is why the rules are
  given here by their text or by a disclosed paraphrase rather than cited by
  number alone.  No rule has been invented and none has been softened.
- The launch configuration audited for the rule on JDK internal access is the
  block at README.md:130-146 in this migrated tree, duplicated at
  docs/setup.md:93-105.  It is described here and cited by anchor, and
  deliberately never reproduced verbatim, for two reasons: it carries
  credential-shaped values, and it also carries pre-existing unbalanced
  quotation marks, which were counted rather than assumed - the single-line
  launch-argument export at README.md:138 holds an odd number of quotation
  marks, and README.md:142 and README.md:143 hold one unmatched mark each.
  Importing that block would copy a pre-existing defect into a new file for no
  benefit, and the rule on discovered defects forbids repairing it, so it is
  neither repaired nor imported.
- Verified in this migrated tree: that launch configuration contains zero
  module-access JVM arguments, and Java 17 needs none.  README.md:148 and
  docs/setup.md:107 both state that positively.  If such an argument ever
  appeared in a captured persistence banner it would be replaced, in this region,
  by the description "a module-access JVM argument that opens or exports an
  otherwise-encapsulated JDK package to the unnamed module", and the substitution
  would be recorded both in this notes block and in the redacted field above.
  Because the audited configuration provably contains none, a faithful capture is
  clean and that fallback should never be needed - it is written down so that a
  future run has a defined action rather than a judgement call.
- Credential posture, in the repository's own voice rather than a policy invented
  here.  docs/setup.md:135 states that the default administrator password is
  intended only for local developer evaluation, that it must be rotated
  immediately in any shared, staging or production deployment, and that
  credentials must be injected through environment variables, encrypted
  configuration values or a secret manager rather than committed to source
  control; that page already renders the administrator password as <REDACTED> at
  :133, where README.md does not.  This capture honours that posture and reuses
  that token.  Measured, not assumed: the documented administrator password
  appears at exactly ONE location tree-wide, in README.md, and its occurrence
  count under this documentation tree, under this smoke-evidence directory and
  under this migrated directory is ZERO.  It is not transcribed anywhere here -
  not even as an illustration of what redaction removes.  No connection URL
  survives in this file either, for the same reason.
- Registered observation, not a defect report and not something to fix: the
  duplicated launch-configuration block at docs/setup.md:93-105 reproduces the
  README's keystore and trust-store password values verbatim rather than
